I know this is vastly too fundamental to be changed at this stage of the
language's development, but why do tanru automatically form? Presumably the
more common a construction is, the fewer cmavo it should take to say it.
(This isn't a direct correlation, though; for example, NU constructions are
very common, but by their nature there is probably no way to avoid having
some cmavo to deal with them.) And certainly {lo gerku cu klama} is more
common than {lo gerku klama cu bajra}. Why wasn't it decided in the ancient
(Loglan?) days to have tanru form only with a cmavo? To clarify, suppose the
cmavo were {ja'ei}* for the default grouping ({ke} and friends would still
work the same way without needing {ja'ei}). Then {lo gerku klama} would be
"the dog goes" while {lo gerku ja'ei klama bajra} would be "the
dog-kind-of-goer runs". Isn't this a bit more sensible than what is
established now?

*Certainly in an actual situation like this, this cmavo would be a CV, since
it would be pretty common. I'm fairly sure there are no CVs to spare now,
though, so it would've been confusing to use one.

mu'o mi'e .latros.

